  {
    "chapter": "changes-have-lifetimes",
    "after_contains": "Identity has to survive these transitions.",
    "html": "<figure><img src=\"assets/walkthrough/removal-fence.svg\" alt=\"A removal timeline shows CPU object removal, cessation of new render references, continued GPU buffer occupancy, a completion fence, and later slot reuse with an incremented generation.\" width=\"1040\" height=\"600\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>The blue allocation remains occupied after the red removal line because previously submitted GPU work may still read it. The fence, rather than the gameplay deletion time, permits physical reuse. Reusing slot 42 with generation 8 prevents an old logical handle for generation 7 from naming the new occupant. This is an illustrative lifetime contract, not a claim that Unreal uses this exact handle encoding.</figcaption></figure>"
  },
  {
    "chapter": "the-scene-becomes-gpu-data",
    "after_contains": "The physical buffer layout is an implementation decision",
    "html": "<figure><img src=\"assets/walkthrough/gpu-byte-address.svg\" alt=\"A contiguous instance buffer is expanded into a 64-byte teaching record with three transform rows and tint; arithmetic obtains address 0x10F0 for instance 3 tint.\" width=\"1040\" height=\"650\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Follow the highlighted fourth record, then its yellow final field. A logical instance ID becomes an address only after applying the stride and field offset. The CPU packing code and GPU decoding code must share that contract. This 64-byte example is deliberately invented for arithmetic clarity; Unreal GPU Scene has its own versioned layouts and additional tables.</figcaption></figure>"
  },
  {
    "chapter": "from-a-proxy-to-a-draw",
    "after_contains": "Before giving Unreal",
    "html": "<figure><img src=\"assets/walkthrough/raster-depth-winners.svg\" alt=\"A numerically rasterized sample grid shows two overlapping triangles, one highlighted shared sample, and the depth comparison that selects the blue triangle.\" width=\"1040\" height=\"660\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>The colored cells are computed from triangle edge tests at their centers; the outlines show the continuous triangles. At sample (4.5,3.5), both cover the sample, then 0.35 wins over 0.60 in this explicitly forward-depth example. The depth convention is stated because copying that inequality into Unreal’s reversed-depth paths would be wrong.</figcaption></figure>"
  },
  {
    "chapter": "the-courtyard-needs-more-than-static-meshes",
    "after_contains": "A ray marcher approximates this integral",
    "html": "<figure><img src=\"assets/walkthrough/alpha-and-volume.svg\" alt=\"Red and blue half-transparent panes are composited in both orders over white, yielding different overlap colors. A computed fog transmittance curve falls from one toward zero as distance increases.\" width=\"1040\" height=\"650\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Read the overlap patches: red over blue gives (0.75,0.25,0.50), while swapping their order gives (0.50,0.25,0.75), using the shown unit-RGB compositing arithmetic over white. A pane is a surface event with an order. Fog instead attenuates continuously along distance: the plotted curve is T(d)=exp(−0.5d). Full volume rendering additionally integrates light scattered and emitted into the ray. Displayed colors illustrate the arithmetic; this is not a complete linear-light display-management example.</figcaption></figure>"
  },
  {
    "chapter": "the-world-is-not-one-tree",
    "after_contains": "For the gate, imagine an actor containing",
    "html": "<figure><img src=\"assets/walkthrough/gate-attachment.svg\" alt=\"A top-down gate swings from zero to ninety degrees about a hinge at 300,200 centimeters. Its lamp is marked at a sixty-centimeter local offset before and after rotation.\" width=\"1040\" height=\"620\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>The brown door becomes the green door while the hinge stays fixed. Both yellow lamp positions are computed from the same parent rotation applied to the lamp’s 60-centimeter local offset. The lamp shares motion ancestry with the door; it does not have to share its mesh asset, material, or draw submission.</figcaption></figure>"
