    "chapter": "motion-is-more-than-a-matrix",
    "after_contains": "Skinning introduces transforms inside the object.",
    "html": "<figure><img src=\"assets/walkthrough/weighted-skinning.svg\" alt=\"Three computed triangulated sleeve strips show rest, previous, and current deformation. The tracked red vertex moves from 2,0 to 2,0.5 to 2,1 under the displayed two-bone weighted calculation.\" width=\"1040\" height=\"670\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>The red vertex is the prose’s 0.75/0.25 example. Its two current transformed positions are (2,0) and (2,4), so the result is (2,1); the previous pose instead used (2,2) for the second result, producing (2,0.5). The strip is actually computed with weights varying along its length. This is an illustrative software deformation using simple bone translations, not an Unreal character capture. Its retained previous result is the information motion reconstruction needs.</figcaption></figure>"
  },
  {
    "chapter": "motion-is-more-than-a-matrix",
    "after_contains": "Bounds must follow the same physical reality.",
    "html": "<figure><img src=\"assets/walkthrough/deformation-bounds.svg\" alt=\"A stick figure outside the camera region extends a hand into it. A stale red dashed bound excludes the hand and fails the visibility test; a larger green current-pose bound intersects the region.\" width=\"1040\" height=\"620\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>The red dashed rest bound lies entirely left of the view boundary, so culling it is internally consistent but visually wrong: the hand has moved into the image. The current green bound includes the hand and survives. A larger correct bound may cost more culling work; a smaller incorrect bound loses visible geometry.</figcaption></figure>"
  },
