  {
    "chapter": "a-visible-surface-becomes-a-lit-pixel",
    "after_contains": "Rasterization first answers",
    "html": "<figure><img src=\"assets/walkthrough/one-pixel-through-the-pipeline.svg\" alt=\"An enlarged projected triangle marks pixel center 230.5,100.5. Three colored barycentric weights interpolate local and world positions, UV and normal. The slab at 400 cm defeats a competing surface at 600 cm; its sampled and tinted material writes base color 0.32,0.30,0.16, world normal 0,0,1 and roughness 0.8.\" width=\"1040\" height=\"980\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Return to <a href=\"#a-triangle-becomes-an-object\">the original 100 cm slab and camera</a>: the same vertices now produce one complete surface sample. The chosen pixel is separate from the earlier small raster-grid exercise. Since all three vertices have the same camera depth, the displayed barycentric weights also give the perspective-correct attributes. Positive view depth selects the nearer sample; this does not describe a hardware depth-buffer encoding. The base-color arithmetic uses linear values, while the color swatch is encoded for display. These are illustrative logical GBuffer fields, before lighting, rather than Unreal’s packed texture layout; depth is a separate visibility result.</figcaption></figure>"
  },
